@startuml
!include ../../../tools/diagrams/kcna-theme.puml
title Secret, ConfigMap, Label — three key-value things that are not interchangeable

rectangle "Secret" <<groupGood>> as sec {
  rectangle "An OBJECT that stores sensitive data\n<size:11>passwords · tokens · keys · certificates</size>\n\n<size:11>data (base64) or stringData (plain, write-only)</size>\n<size:11>has a TYPE: Opaque by default, plus seven built-ins</size>\n<size:11>1 MiB · same namespace as the Pod</size>\n\n<size:11>READ BY: the container, via env, envFrom or a volume</size>\n<size:11>values are redacted in kubectl describe</size>" <<good>> as s
}

rectangle "ConfigMap" <<group>> as cm {
  rectangle "An OBJECT that stores non-sensitive config\n<size:11>URLs · feature flags · whole config files</size>\n\n<size:11>data (UTF-8) or binaryData (base64)</size>\n<size:11>NO type field</size>\n<size:11>1 MiB · same namespace as the Pod</size>\n\n<size:11>READ BY: the container, via env, envFrom or a volume</size>\n<size:11>values are shown in full by kubectl describe</size>" <<plain>> as c
}

rectangle "Label" <<groupBad>> as lbl {
  rectangle "NOT storage at all — metadata ON an object\n<size:11>app=nginx · tier=frontend · env=prod</size>\n\n<size:11>lives in metadata.labels of any object</size>\n<size:11>values capped at 63 characters</size>\n\n<size:11>READ BY: the CONTROL PLANE, to SELECT objects —</size>\n<size:11>a Service's selector, a Deployment's matchLabels,</size>\n<size:11>a DaemonSet's nodeSelector, kubectl get -l</size>\n<size:11>the container never sees them unless you inject them</size>" <<bad>> as l
}

sec -[hidden]right-> cm
cm -[hidden]right-> lbl

rectangle "Why two nearly identical objects?  The author of both features:\n<size:11>“Use Secrets for things which are actually secret like API keys, credentials, etc. Use ConfigMaps for not-secret configuration data.</size>\n<size:11>In general, we like intent-based APIs, and the intent is definitely different for secret data vs. plain old configs.”</size>\n\n<size:11>The intent is not cosmetic: it decides what RBAC rule covers it, whether encryption at rest applies, whether it is held in tmpfs,</size>\n<size:11>whether it is redacted in output, and whether a reviewer notices it in a pull request.</size>" <<muted>> as why

cm -[hidden]down-> why

caption <i>The exam contrast: Secret and ConfigMap are both key-value OBJECTS a Pod consumes, differing in intent and handling. A Label is neither — it is metadata used to find and group objects, never to feed data to an application.</i>\n<i>If a question asks where to put a database password, it is a Secret; a log level, a ConfigMap; “which Pods belong to this Service”, a Label.</i>
@enduml
